From 92398b696d58d724c5d0c0188568d15d69902db4 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sat, 11 Apr 2026 17:18:29 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 8/9] ktranslate-topology: live web viewer for SNMP topology
 data
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Adds a standalone viewer binary at cmd/ktranslate-topology that turns
KSnmpTopology JCHF records emitted by the main ktranslate binary into
an auto-refreshing web page showing devices, their interfaces, and the
links between them.

Architecture:

- graph.go — in-memory store with Device -> Interface -> Link nodes.
  Links are canonicalised by sorting the (device,interface) endpoints so
  reciprocal views (A->B plus B->A) collapse into a single edge tagged
  with the union of their discovery sources. A Snapshot() method
  prunes stale links (older than the configurable -ttl flag) and emits
  a nodes+edges document for the browser.

- ingest.go — parses a kt.JCHF-shaped JSON payload (the exact shape
  ktranslate's -format=json sink produces), filters to topology
  records, handles gzip bodies, and applies each observation to the
  graph. Declared its own local struct instead of importing pkg/kt so
  the viewer stays free of the gosnmp / leveldb dependency chain.

- server.go — net/http handler with POST /ingest, GET /graph.json,
  GET /healthz, and GET / for the static HTML viewer. The HTML file is
  embedded via //go:embed so the binary has no runtime file dependency.

- assets/index.html — single-file, self-contained viewer built on
  vanilla JS + SVG. Implements a small force-directed layout (node
  repulsion, edge springs, interface-to-device "glue" springs, centre
  pull) so no JS framework or CDN asset is required — the page works
  fully offline. Devices are drawn as blue nodes with their interfaces
  clustered around them; link colour reflects the discovery protocol
  (LLDP green, CDP yellow, both blue).

- main.go — flag parsing (-listen, -ttl), graceful shutdown on
  SIGINT/SIGTERM, thin plumbing.

Tests cover link dedup / source merge, TTL pruning and orphaning,
missing-field dropping, JSON ingest (array and single-object bodies),
gzip, neighbor_source "lldp+cdp" parsing, and every HTTP endpoint.

Wiring it up is a one-line change to ktranslate's sinks:

  ktranslate -format json -sinks http \
    -http_url http://localhost:8082/ingest ...
  ktranslate-topology -listen :8082

See the Live topology viewer section in vendor/ktranslate/README.md
for details.
